        <body>
            <div xml:id="d198">

                <pb facs="0466" n="438" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v03/pg438"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> Hickerson–Murphy–Key files, lot 58 D 33,
                    “Secretary Generalship of UN”</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the United Nations
                        Adviser, Bureau of European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Allen</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1953-02-19">February 19,
                        1953</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <item>Subject:</item>
                    <item>Length of GA; Secretary Generalship</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <item>Participants:</item>
                    <item>Mr. Georges Carlier, Counselor, Belgian Embassy</item>
                    <item>Mr. Ward Allen, EUR</item>
                </list>
                <p>Ambassador Zaroubin volunteered the following comments to Baron Silvercruys this
                    morning as firm predictions: <list>
                        <label>1)</label>
                        <item>The GA session will last at least through Easter;</item>
                        <label>2)</label>
                        <item> Trygve Lie will continue as Secretary-General through the expiration
                            of his present term. This, according to Zaroubin, is Lie’s dearest wish.
                            Zaroubin strongly denied reports that the USSR has indicated it would
                            accept Pearson, but, of course, refused to say whether or not they would
                            veto him.</item>
                    </list></p>
            </div>
        </body>
